#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125774 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadric surface with the equation\n\n$$z = x^2 + y^2$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14e8d23a4bc649257c2f7351e0d2c1d62e39a8cd8eed1467e65853ee121b836e.png)\n\nThis surface is an example of an **elliptic paraboloid.**\n\nThe **vertex** of this elliptic paraboloid is located at the origin, and its **axis of symmetry** is the $z$-axis. Note that the axis of symmetry is the vertical line that passes through the vertex.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis whose vertex is at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$z - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nThese surfaces are called *elliptic* *paraboloids* because their traces in the $xy$-plane are *ellipses*, while their traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly." :tutorial/id #uuid "d5da7d20-bcec-546d-8cd6-178215fc2096" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3439 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125776 :tutorial/content "Now consider the graph of the quadric surface\n\n$$z = x^2 -y^2$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f02c207742c737d00cdeabbd2cbba93a88a42521c41a2cc1e9f19d27ff55f20.png)\n\nThis surface is an example of a **hyperbolic paraboloid** whose **saddle point** is at the origin.\n\nMore generally, a hyperbolic paraboloid whose saddle point is at $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ has the general equation $$z - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2}- \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ constants.\n\nThis surface is called *hyperbolic* *paraboloid* because its traces in the $xy$-plane are *hyperbolas*, while its traces in the $yz$- and $xz$-planes are *parabolas*. We'll discuss the traces of this surface in more detail shortly.\n\nAnother property is that this surface mirrors itself across the planes $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Since these planes meet along the $z$-axis, that axis serves as the **axis of symmetry** of the hyperbolic paraboloid." :tutorial/id #uuid "15370709-5e87-5dcb-8a1f-ff66b0bf86e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3442 :tutorial/title "Hyperbolic Paraboloids"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125805 :tutorial/content "So far, we've encountered elliptic paraboloids whose axes of symmetry are parallel to the $z$-axis. Let's now consider the cases when an elliptic paraboloid is oriented along the $x$- or the $y$-axes.\n\nFor an elliptic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows.\n\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis: $$y = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}$$\n\n![](images/da/da37415ddd27764a719fecc5780ee91e6e8ad3f5ce877101b81d1a955c57eb6f.png)\n- When the vertex is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis: $$x = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}$$\n\n![](images/d1/d1488d9f4767312795a84ce6441f64dd567223d1de4da66adbd6e39de9612a53.png)\n\nLikewise, for a hyperbolic paraboloid, the other two cases are as follows:\n\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $y$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $y$-axis. The two corresponding equations are $$y = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}.$$\n- When the saddle point is at the origin and the $x$ term is linear, the axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis. The two corresponding equations are $$x = \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad x = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f53539f-e8db-5205-b9d0-3919805097ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3851 :tutorial/title "Alternative Orientations of the Axis of Symmetry"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125880 :tutorial/content "The traces of an elliptic paraboloid are either ellipses, parabolas, a single point, or the empty set.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the elliptic paraboloid given by\n\n$$z=x^2+y^2.$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/02/021dd988863021f564a0e85fb3db93030740059f2a50ade6504b3e12a5665372.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ {x}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}$$ which is a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/9f/9f6cd0703b5d7b2458cbfd688d91f08a79752fa1ccd3616e5975f5c017aa7b42.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ {y}^{2} & =z,\\end{aligned}$$ which is a parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/89/89be283ed121d3e30b1913e4412b8d3556ed611ef2a9a9bfd686d0594409f5c9.png)\n\nThere are a few other cases we should note:\n\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=-k$ for $k > 0$ is the empty set. This is because $z\\geq 0$ everywhere on the elliptic paraboloid.\n- The trace of our surface with the plane $z=k$ for $k > 0$ is an ellipse: $$x^2+y^2 = k$$\n\n![](images/f1/f15e925431b0d2ccfd4afa703cad1bcb37b65a75e96fbeabcf4823703bf184e5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0ea2e49f-f5aa-57b1-9b67-de6947a0e6e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7839 :tutorial/title "Traces of Elliptic Paraboloids"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125881 :tutorial/content "The traces of a hyperbolic paraboloid are either hyperbolas, parabolas, or intersecting lines.\n\nFor example, let's find the traces with the coordinate planes for the hyperbolic paraboloid given by\n\n$$z=x^2-y^2.$$\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm x\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to two intersecting lines in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/9b/9b3fbb9d8945c5d9208a59d404e489e4a339082c602c18154010e3e53083b220.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & ={x}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to the parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/e8/e8e2b6306052e781fd149e4eebb04da79eddb44b5261b05b129f208ad523b70b.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation $$\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =z \\\\ z & =-{y}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$ which corresponds to the parabola in the $yz$-plane that has its vertex at the origin.\n\n![](images/70/7010e4c26af60c6c27f66c93b805a2bac1e191c1646b5aac3a77f5dd3dadc64f.png)\n\nIn this particular case, the **saddle point** is at the origin. It is a minimum point for the trace in the $xz$-plane but a maximum point for the trace in the $yz$-plane.\n\nFinally, note that the trace of our surface with the plane $z=k\\neq 0$ is a hyperbola: $$x^2-y^2 = k$$\n\n![](images/b9/b9c1baab2f9d85c2c415cae91908d5c908aa0b1cef1eb8b5901f7f3b78bf66b2.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2467f92-856a-5690-bd8d-1c593268396a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7840 :tutorial/title "Traces of Hyperbolic Paraboloids"}]}
